text:
Replace the content of references.bib with:

@misc{interview_john2026,
  author = {Smith, John},
  title = {Interview on AI adoption},
  year = {2026},
  note = {IT manager, mid-sized company (fictitious data)}
}

@misc{interview_mary2026,
  author = {Jones, Mary},
  title = {Interview on technology resistance},
  year = {2026},
  note = {Systems analyst, startup (fictitious data)}
}






Real interviews



Do not record participants’ names. Use neutral keys (int01, int02) and remove identifying data from the texts.

With the graph in Neo4j, the mcp-neo4j-cypher MCP server lets you query it in natural language from Claude Desktop. In claude_desktop_config.json:
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "synesis-neo4j": {
      "command": "uvx",
      "args": ["mcp-neo4j-cypher@0.5.2", "--read-only"],
      "env": {
        "NEO4J_URI": "bolt://localhost:7687",
        "NEO4J_USERNAME": "neo4j",
        "NEO4J_PASSWORD": "your_password",
        "NEO4J_DATABASE": "database_name"
      }
    }
  }
}
The file is in %APPDATA%\Claude\ (Windows) or ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/ (macOS). Keep --read-only. The repository ships a full guide in the mcp/ folder.

synesis compile analysis.synp --csv out/
out/chains.csv, one row per triple:
bibref,from_code,relation,to_code,source_file,source_line,source_column
'@interview_01,high_cost,INHIBITS,adoption,annotations.syn,8,5



Each item of corpus carries its chains, and indices.triples gathers all of them, with their origin:
{
  "indices": {
    "triples": [
      {
        "from": "high_cost",
        "relation": "INHIBITS",
        "to": "adoption",
        "source_item": "interview_01_item0001",
        "location": {"file": "annotations.syn", "line": 8, "column": 5}
      }
    ]
  }
}

Create a project folder:
mkdir telemedicine-analysis
cd telemedicine-analysis
Create references.bib with the references of the interviews. The keys are neutral and do not identify participants:

@misc{interview01,
  author = {Participant 01},
  title = {Interview on access to health care},
  year = {2026},
  note = {Resident of a rural community, 45 minutes (fictitious data)}
}

@misc{interview02,
  author = {Participant 02},
  title = {Interview on technology and health},
  year = {2026},
  note = {Resident of a rural community, 60 minutes (fictitious data)}
}

@misc{interview03,
  author = {Participant 03},
  title = {Interview, professional perspective},
  year = {2026},
  note = {Rural physician, 40 minutes (fictitious data)}
}






Checkpoint ✓



You should have the folder telemedicine-analysis/ with the file references.bib and your 3 interviews.
